**W1-D4-Class_Methods_Employee**

---

**What and Why:**
Until now every attribute (`self.name`, `self.balance`) belonged to one specific object. Today you learn **class variables** — data shared across ALL objects, not separate per object. Also learning `@classmethod` — a method that works on the class itself, not one object. This pattern appears in Django managers (`Post.objects.create()`, `Post.objects.filter()`) — called on the class, not one object.

---

```python
# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 1: CLASS VARIABLE VS INSTANCE VARIABLE
# ============================================================

class Employee:
    total_employees = 0
    # CLASS VARIABLE — lives on the CLASS itself, not on any object
    # shared by every Employee object ever created
    # think of it as a shared counter on the wall everyone can see
    # defined at class level, not inside __init__

    company_name = "TechCorp"
    # another class variable — same value for every employee
    # accessible without creating any object: Employee.company_name

    def __init__(self, name, salary):
        self.name = name
        # INSTANCE VARIABLE — belongs to this specific object only
        # car1 has its own name, car2 has its own name — separate
        self.salary = salary
        # instance variable — separate per object

        Employee.total_employees += 1
        # every time new Employee created, increase shared counter by 1
        # Employee.total_employees — accessing CLASS variable using class name
        # NOT self.total_employees — that would create separate copy per object
        # that's a common bug — explained below


# ============================================================
# CONCEPT 2: @CLASSMETHOD
# ============================================================

    @classmethod
    def get_total_employees(cls):
        # @classmethod decorator — tells Python this works on CLASS not object
        # cls = the class itself (Employee), NOT self (one object)
        # cls is automatically passed by Python, same as self for instance methods
        return cls.total_employees
        # reads shared counter from the class

    @classmethod
    def from_string(cls, data_string):
        # alternate constructor — creates Employee from different input format
        # useful when data comes from file, API response, CSV etc
        # data_string example: "Ravi-30000"

        name, salary = data_string.split("-")
        # split("-") breaks "Ravi-30000" into ["Ravi", "30000"]
        # name = "Ravi", salary = "30000" (still string)

        return cls(name, int(salary))
        # int(salary) converts "30000" string to 30000 number
        # cls(name, int(salary)) = Employee(name, int(salary))
        # creates and returns new Employee object


# ============================================================
# CREATING OBJECTS AND TESTING
# ============================================================

e1 = Employee("Ravi", 30000)
e2 = Employee("Sai", 40000)
e3 = Employee.from_string("Kiran-28000")
# created using classmethod — useful when input is a string

print(Employee.get_total_employees())  # 3
print(Employee.company_name)           # TechCorp — no object needed
print(e3.name, e3.salary)             # Kiran 28000
```

